    "text": "Two identical capacitors&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math>&nbsp;of capacitance&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>C</mi></math>&nbsp;each,&nbsp;are charged to the same potential&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>V</mi></math> and are connected in two circuits at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></math>, as shown in the figure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>ii</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;respectively. The charge on the capacitors at time<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&nbsp;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>R</mi></math> are respectively<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/ci/image6-0bfe2523-acb2-4c36-b44b-a7f6a447c810.jpg\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 106px;\" />",
